																		Unknown elements...
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/terralthra" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1293846); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1293846&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606639415" alt="Terralthra" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1293846)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/terralthra" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1293846); return false;" >Terralthra</a></span> <span class="odate time_1336034877 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">03 May 2012 08:47</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1436255">
									

<p>Using &quot;elements that aren't on the periodic table!&quot; always screams &quot;does not understand basic chemistry/physics.&quot; There aren't any empty spaces on the periodic table until atomic numbers &gt; 118. The gaps between Hydrogen and Helium don't indicate that there's something in between, they're there so that elements which behave similarly share columns (hydrogen with the alkali metals, helium with the noble gases). Elements above Iron (Fe, 26) are atomically unstable, tending to decay over time inexorably toward Iron, releasing energy as they do. The time it takes for them to decay goes down precipitously the higher the atomic number, with the highest ones there having half-lives measurable only in fractions of a millisecond, and only ever created (as far as we know) with immense apparati of particle accelerators and fusors.</p>
<p>Chemical compounds involving these elements are extraordinarily unlikely (due to the high energy necessary to create them, ionic and covalent bonds would be highly improbable to form, and by the time the energy levels could possibly decrease, the atoms in question would've decayed into something else). Any new element on the periodic table would have to be an even higher atomic number, hence even faster-decaying, hence even less likely to make a compound, ever. The only way that could work is to theorize that for some reason, once you get past the series of elements which decay faster and faster, there's some point at which you hit an &quot;island of stability&quot; in the ridiculously high atomic numbers. All the evidence thus far provides no support to the hypothesis that such an island exists.</p>
<p>If you want to impress upon the reader that this SCP knows about compounds the Foundation can't duplicate, there are more plausible ways of going about it. Complex compounds involving noble gases, for example, are extremely rare: Neon has no known neutral compounds, helium has only a few hypothetical ones, and the rest have only a few hundred possible compounds combined. Making compounds with those would require tremendous technical proficiency, advanced chemistry knowledge, and skillful manipulation, while still being plausible.</p>

							</div>

								
					
