			<div class="content" id="post-content-1436860">
									

<p>Point of order: The &quot;islands of stability&quot; in high-number elements that Terralthra dismises so readily do theoretically exist. Particularly, whenever you get to the end of a new periodic table row, you would come to a new noble gas. We haven't gotten there yet, but there is every reason to believe that the new nobles will have the same inert stability as their more common cousins. There are a few other places where particle physics collides with chemistry to make the relevant eggheads think a usefully long-lived element may be formed. But I'm too lazy to try and find the Popular Science article on the whole thing right now.</p>
<p>Enough real science, now for the sci-fi reasoning. Right now, large atoms are formed by slamming smaller atoms into each other at stupidly high speeds. This is like assembling legos by shooting them at each other out of rifles. Yes, they stick together, but is it any surpirse that the resulting amalgamation is unstable? Now imagine that we get smart enough to combine nuclei at much lower energy levels (i.e. not a head-on collision at speeds measured in fractions of the speed of light). Insert &quot;heisenberg compensator&quot; black-box technology here. This new &quot;cold&quot; atomic synthesis could very well lead to trans-uranics with a stable, chemically-compoundable, shelf-life. Maybe the magic dots from the super-lab know something about molecular synthesis that we don't. Just saying.</p>
<p>We now return you to something you actually care about.</p>

