<blockquote>
<p>Point of order: The &quot;islands of stability&quot; in high-number elements that Terralthra dismises so readily do theoretically exist. Particularly, whenever you get to the end of a new periodic table row, you would come to a new noble gas. We haven't gotten there yet, but there is every reason to believe that the new nobles will have the same inert stability as their more common cousins. There are a few other places where particle physics collides with chemistry to make the relevant eggheads think a usefully long-lived element may be formed. But I'm too lazy to try and find the Popular Science article on the whole thing right now.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Not to get overly nitpicky, but chemically <em>inert</em> is not the same thing as atomically <em>stable</em>. Unuoctium (atomic number 118, theoretically a member of group 18, the noble gases) still has a half-life measured in milliseconds. Of note, due to relativistic effects, unuoctium is not actually a noble gas, being solid at room temperature and theoretically reactive with electron-donating elements (e.g. hydrogen) or with ring-formations of reactive gases (e.g. UuoF2, UuoF4).</p>
<p>I don't readily dismiss them, I said they could theoretically exist. The phrase &quot;do theoretically exist&quot; is not really an accurate way of phrasing it. Some theories propose such an island exists, other theories do not. Without any observation, the existing trend of atomic stability doesn't point in a positive direction. As a side point, even the theories that do propose an island of high atomic-mass stability put forth that they are high only by comparison with existing high-atomic mass elements; they would still have half-lives measurable in seconds, days at the most.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Enough real science, now for the sci-fi reasoning. Right now, large atoms are formed by slamming smaller atoms into each other at stupidly high speeds. This is like assembling legos by shooting them at each other out of rifles. Yes, they stick together, but is it any surpirse that the resulting amalgamation is unstable? Now imagine that we get smart enough to combine nuclei at much lower energy levels (i.e. not a head-on collision at speeds measured in fractions of the speed of light). Insert &quot;heisenberg compensator&quot; black-box technology here. This new &quot;cold&quot; atomic synthesis could very well lead to trans-uranics with a stable, chemically-compoundable, shelf-life. Maybe the magic dots from the super-lab know something about molecular synthesis that we don't. Just saying.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is also technically inaccurate. Elements with high atomic numbers are not unstable due to the circumstances of their creation, but due to interactions of the strong and weak forces on the nucleus. This is borne out experimentally, as when we fuse hydrogen into helium (involving the same incredibly high energy levels), the resulting helium is just as stable as helium found naturally. Further, astrophysics research supports the notion that all elements heavier than hydrogen were originally formed by stellar fusion processes, operating in the cores of older, larger stars which had run out of hydrogen to fuse and instead fused helium into boron, carbon, oxygen, silicon, and so on up to iron. When these stars novaed (or supernovaed), these elements (and even heavy elements like uranium) were scattered throughout the cosmos, to eventually gravitationally accrete into planets made up chiefly of elements such as iron and silicon. Despite their formation in the hearts of a star, at millions of degrees Celsius, with pressures measurable in millions or billions of atmospheres, the atoms formed in these reactions have been stable enough for Terra to be here for 4.5 billion years and counting. The circumstances of formation are not the determining factor in atomic stability and nuclear decay, the mass of the nucleus is.</p>

